               SENATE COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION
                     Dede Alpert, Chair
                 1999-2000 Regular Session
                              

BILL NO:       AB 1
AUTHOR:        Aanestad
AMENDED:       May 28, 1999
FISCAL COMM:   Yes            HEARING DATE:  June 16, 1999
URGENCY:       Yes            CONSULTANT:James Wilson



 <u> SUMMARY </u> 

This bill, an urgency measure, extends the repeal date on a  
statute that allows Plumas County to receive necessary  
small school and necessary small high school funding.

 <u> BACKGROUND </u> 

Current law provides a minimum "floor" of funding for  
individual schools if those schools enroll fewer than  
specified numbers of pupils and are in school districts  
that enroll a total of less than 2,501 pupils.  These  
"necessary small schools" and "necessary small high  
schools" receive specified allowances based upon the size  
of the small school as measured by number of teachers and  
students, and in lieu of revenue limit funding that would  
usually be allocated per pupil in attendance.

Several school districts have been statutorily allowed to  
receive necessary small school allowances based upon local  
circumstances and in spite of the general qualification  
requirements.  Since 1997, schools in Plumas County Unified  
have been funded through the necessary small school  
formulas in spite of the fact that Plumas has a total of  
more than 2,501 pupils.  The 1997 legislation provided for  
the Plumas County allowance to become inoperative July 1,  
1999 and repeal on January 1, 2000.

 <u>ANALYSIS

This bill, an urgency measure:
 </u> 
1)   Allows Plumas County Unified to continue to receive  




                                                        AB 1
                                                      Page 2



     necessary small school funding for three additional  
     years by revising the sunset clause in current law so  
     that the County's allowance will become inoperative on  
     July 1, 2002 and repealed on January 1, 2003.

2)   Requires the Plumas County District to report to the  
     Departments of Education and Finance by July 1, 2001  
     regarding the district's plan to meet its need for  
     funds following the repeal of the small school  
     allowance.


 <u> STAFF COMMENTS </u> 

 <u> Plumas County Unified argues that it has unique  
circumstances that justify continued authorization of the  
high level of funding. </u>  The district once attempted to  
break into smaller districts, each of which would have  
qualified for the higher level of funding.  District  
breakup was denied by the State Board of Education,  
however, on the grounds that it would hurt the education  
program of the districts.   Closing schools is also not an  
option due to the even higher costs that would have to be  
incurred to transport students.  Finally, the district has  
lost substantial federal forest reserve funding in recent  
years.

 <u> SUPPORT </u> 

Plumas County Teachers Association
Plumas County Unified School District
Regional Council of Rural Counties
Several letters from individuals at Chester High School

 <u> OPPOSITION </u> 

None received <u> 
